# O11F-OBSERVE acceptance (tests/probes/listening-o11fo-acceptance.py), written by the independent acceptance author.
# This is the TARGET: every check passes on the finished outcome. On main 6f9c7a3 (2.7.0) the report does not exist, so
# N1, A1-A4, N2, X1-X5 (cross-process), G1-G4, L1-L4, R1-R6, P1, T1-T4 and C1 fail; N1b, A0, A7, A8 (the probe's own ground truth and the
# no-side-effect checks), the child-health checks and T5-T8 (source_tree behaviours that already hold) pass.
# Author-set limits (flagged for a coordinator ruling): 20 s shed wait, 4 rounds x 3 loops, 10 bounces, reference count 1..64.
# Open interface choices the oracles read by meaning: field names, "how many loops have shed" as events or distinct loops.
# X1-X5 (coordinator ruling, phase 1): roomctl run as a separate process through the shipped script and a real socket.
# Phase 2 (60d5b63 merged): K0-K5 (SIGKILLed window, new window, two windows, stale/garbage files beside live ones) and J1-J3 (planted files, no window) added at the coordinator's request.
# Revisions: the parser reads the ruled `state` names (gtk, no_libgstgl, refused, no_player, unreported are "not active"); checkpoint reads wait up to 3 s (author-set) for the report to catch up with the real counts, the status being saved on a worker thread.
N1 with no window ever opened, inspect is readable (exit 0, one JSON line) and has a shedding report that does not claim shedding is active
N1b inspect did not start the shedding machinery: the GStreamer calls it would load are untouched
X3 as a separate process (shipped roomctl.py over the real socket) with no window open, inspect is readable and has a shedding report that does not claim shedding is active
A0 fixture: at least 3 catalogue loop rooms with distinct loop files
A1 with a loop on screen and nothing refused, inspect reports scene-loop shedding active
X1 as a separate process (shipped roomctl.py over the real socket) with a loop on screen, inspect reports scene-loop shedding active
A8 under constant inspect polling the loops still shed once per leave and resume once per show (the probe's own count of real sheds and resumes matches the workload)
A2 the report counts the loops that have shed, and the count matches real sheds at every checkpoint over 4 rounds of 3 loops
A3 the report counts the loops that have resumed, and the count matches real resumes at every checkpoint (a first show is not a resume)
X2 as a separate process, the shed and resumed counts match real sheds and resumes at every checkpoint over 4 rounds of 3 loops
A4 after 10 quick show/leave pairs the report follows the real sheds and resumes, not the clicks
A7 inspect never changes shedding: 40 inspects with loops shed leave every loop's state, the shed and resume calls and the shedding hooks as they were, and nothing wakes afterwards
N2 with the window closed, inspect is still readable (exit 0, one JSON line) and has a shedding report whose counts, if it gives them, are not above what really happened
X5 as a separate process with the window closed, inspect is still readable and has a shedding report whose counts, if it gives them, are not above what really happened
active: the child finished without a crash or hang
active: the child reported all its oracles and exited 0
G1 gate: with a different GTK version, inspect reports shedding not active and the reason names GTK
G2 gate: the counts are there and zero (no loop shed or resumed), and the loops still play
G3 gate: after loops are shown and left again the report is unchanged: not active, same reason, counts zero
G4 gate: the report is plain language
gate: the child finished without a crash or hang
gate: the child reported all its oracles and exited 0
L1 gl: with libgstgl missing, inspect reports shedding not active and the reason names libgstgl
L2 gl: the counts are there and zero (no loop shed or resumed), and the loops still play
L3 gl: after loops are shown and left again the report is unchanged: not active, same reason, counts zero
L4 gl: the report is plain language
gl: the child finished without a crash or hang
gl: the child reported all its oracles and exited 0
R1 refs: before the release is refused, inspect does not say shedding is off
R2 refs: after the refused release, inspect reports shedding not active and the reason names references
R3 refs: the report shows the observed reference count (a whole number from 1 to 64)
R4 refs: the counts agree with what happened (at most the loop whose release was refused shed; resumes never above sheds)
R5 refs: the loops still play and resume, and afterwards shedding is still reported off for the same reason
R6 refs: the report is plain language
X4 refs: as a separate process (shipped roomctl.py over the real socket), inspect reports shedding not active, the reason names references and shows the observed count (a whole number from 1 to 64)
refs: the child finished without a crash or hang
refs: the child reported all its oracles and exited 0
J1 a status file whose pid is dead, alone: inspect is readable, does not claim shedding is active and reports none of its counts
J2 a stale file whose pid was reused by an unrelated live process (not a Listening Room window): inspect is readable and makes no false active claim and reports none of its counts
J3 corrupt, truncated, empty, binary, wrong-typed, unknown-state, oversized, impossible-pid and odd-named status files (and a directory named like one), each alone: inspect stays readable (exit 0, one JSON line) and makes no active claim
junk: the child finished without a crash or hang
junk: the child reported all its oracles and exited 0
K1 a window running in another process: inspect (in the window process and as a separate roomctl process) reports it active with its real counts
K0 harness precondition: a window killed with SIGKILL leaves its status file behind
K2 after the window was killed, inspect does not claim shedding is active and reports none of the dead window's counts
K3 a new window after the kill: inspect reports the new window (active, its own real counts), not the dead one's
K4 two windows at once: inspect reports one window, the newest, with its own counts (not a sum or a mix)
K5 files from a dead pid (newer than the live windows') and corrupt files beside live windows do not hide or replace the newest live window's report
stale: the child finished without a crash or hang
stale: the child reported all its oracles and exited 0
P1 the three "not active" reasons are all given and differ from each other (another GTK version, no libgstgl, refused release)
T1 source_tree: the --source tree is also on PYTHONPATH and LISTENING_HUB_SOURCE names another tree: the --source tree wins (and LISTENING_HUB_SOURCE follows it)
T2 source_tree: the --source tree is also on PYTHONPATH and LISTENING_HUB_SOURCE is unset: the --source tree is used, not the default tree
T3 source_tree: the same when --source is spelled differently from the PYTHONPATH entry (trailing slash)
T4 source_tree: the same when PYTHONPATH lists the --source tree after the default tree
T5 source_tree: the dev loop's PYTHONPATH export alone is not a choice: LISTENING_HUB_SOURCE names another tree and that tree wins
T6 source_tree: the PYTHONPATH export alone, no LISTENING_HUB_SOURCE: the default tree
T7 source_tree: another tree only exported on PYTHONPATH (never put first on sys.path by the caller) is not a choice either: LISTENING_HUB_SOURCE wins
T8 source_tree: control, a --source tree that is not on PYTHONPATH still wins over LISTENING_HUB_SOURCE
C1 the CHANGELOG [Unreleased] section records the inspect addition: scene-loop shedding status
